<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
    Entitlements for the macOS sandbox broker.

    com.apple.developer.endpoint-security.client is a *restricted* entitlement: Apple grants it to a
    named team, and the kernel refuses to create an Endpoint Security client without it
    (ES_NEW_CLIENT_RESULT_ERR_NOT_ENTITLED). A signature carrying it is only honoured when it comes
    from a provisioning profile Apple issued, or when the machine has been told to stop checking -
    SIP disabled and amfi_get_out_of_my_way=1, which is how this is tested without the grant.

    The build signs the broker with these entitlements ad-hoc so a development machine produces a
    broker that works. A shipping build has to re-sign with the team's real identity; ad-hoc is not a
    substitute for the grant, it just makes the entitlement present so a relaxed machine honours it.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>com.apple.developer.endpoint-security.client</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
